#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8311c425-96db-50b4-bda2-7afc21298e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261013} {:answer/id #uuid "970d51ce-6cc7-567e-b921-0a5f575739b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261184} {:answer/id #uuid "aed5ed6e-37c8-5e3c-ae55-c95e30ce3202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261416} {:answer/id #uuid "bc52c38c-023f-560d-827e-ce988bae6a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261533} {:answer/id #uuid "c912ae60-77c2-546c-be59-6f64006178e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc52c38c-023f-560d-827e-ce988bae6a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261533} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c61931c-b6de-57e6-b862-b7df723296cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22d6e6f4-daf0-5645-a375-f0c335545e70" :question/math-academy-id "q-126902" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}-t,5-3{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨-1,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t-1,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,2⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,2⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t-1,-6t⟩ & =c⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t-1,-6t⟩ & =⟨-c,2c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4t-1=-c \\\\ -6t=2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=-4t+1.$ Substituting $c=-4t+1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6t & =2(-4t+1) \\\\ -6t & =-8t+2 \\\\ 2t & =2 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨-1,2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{2}-1,5-3(1)^{2}⟩=⟨1,2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a91054f-adf7-5f56-83c2-fa3119dc478c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282606} {:answer/id #uuid "88ea9708-0042-5422-b8c9-124b38c78848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(64,2)$ and $(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282871} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc619de-3849-50d7-a032-ed5d0fa3212b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(64,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283469} {:answer/id #uuid "dc37b06f-ca47-55ac-9f76-e24057ce58fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\,,\\,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283609} {:answer/id #uuid "f38a4e4e-a50c-5a8c-9c70-c08d603abc61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{16},-\\frac{7}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc37b06f-ca47-55ac-9f76-e24057ce58fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\,,\\,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283609} :answer-field/id #uuid "109ae18e-4733-5298-895a-59e0b555d56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "854e79dd-e49f-5d57-896b-a1669e5ffce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-164833" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{5}\\,,\\,{t}^{2}-2⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{5})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 10{t}^{4}+(-5)\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 10{t}^{4}-10t & =0 \\\\ 10t({t}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{5}\\,,\\,(1)^{2}-2⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2\\,,\\,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "580c3843-6361-5a93-8e93-aa4eecc3f021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24,26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282438} {:answer/id #uuid "95ec2ae6-b848-5b2c-9e73-71480e6476c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-9,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282983} {:answer/id #uuid "9978da76-99f6-51aa-835d-d6470ba9b397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283023} {:answer/id #uuid "c39a49da-d2c5-5e18-8270-20d823e49792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-9,-4)$ and $(-8,-6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283395} {:answer/id #uuid "ca04d778-71e9-52fd-ba77-a58b5aaf9a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,-4)$ and $(24,26)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "580c3843-6361-5a93-8e93-aa4eecc3f021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24,26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282438} :answer-field/id #uuid "543918ac-60ea-556f-87e9-9fe723e51c77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a127c57-e392-5180-8b85-86041bead0c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-165038" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{5}-8,2{t}^{4}-6⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5}-8),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =⟨c,\\frac{4c}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{4}=c \\\\ 8{t}^{3}=\\frac{4c}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=5{t}^{4}.$ Substituting $c=5{t}^{4}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{t}^{3} & =\\frac{4(5{t}^{4})}{5} \\\\ 8{t}^{3}-4{t}^{4} & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3}(2-t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=2.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨(2)^{5}-8,2(2)^{4}-6⟩=⟨24,26⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(24,26).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bdb4010-1e08-5378-a110-856c1b0d31e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342732} {:answer/id #uuid "8844755e-17ca-5997-8087-0445418bac62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343418} {:answer/id #uuid "97e8fdd1-1609-5108-82df-e53e59510619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343570} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b6012c-303d-5b23-b10c-010c9c93ca49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,-1,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344097} {:answer/id #uuid "fd335b51-418d-5e75-99ad-992ab400d4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bdb4010-1e08-5378-a110-856c1b0d31e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342732} :answer-field/id #uuid "c171024e-4b6d-5601-9cbd-861fac1471b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a928b0be-2c4b-5a25-9350-6790b8cc0365" :question/math-academy-id "q-75203" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}]}
